# IDE and local tool state
.vscode/
.idea/

# Local tooling state used to be ignored BY SHAPE (a blanket `/.*/` matching every dotted directory at
# the root, with .github//.githooks//.anvil/ re-included by name) rather than by naming what's actually
# local. REPLACED 2026-08-27 (ANVIL step 1, D0065): a blanket default is a trap for anything created
# later. `.anvil/` proved it can go either way in the same round it landed -- caught here, this time,
# only because `check_untracked_files.py` (gate 27) existed to catch it; `.github/`, `.githooks/`,
# `.claude/`'s commands/ predate that gate and were verified tracked as intended only by checking now, not
# by the shape rule reliably protecting them. A blanket ignore-by-shape rule and a gate that fails loudly
# on anything ungoverned are opposites: the first makes a new local-tooling directory silently vanish from
# a fresh clone; the second makes it a build failure until someone decides, explicitly, whether it ships
# or not. Gate 27 is the correct mechanism now that it exists, so the blanket rule is redundant with it at
# best and a silent single point of failure at worst -- removed rather than kept "just in case."
#
# What's actually local, named directly instead of inferred from a leading dot: `.godot/`/`.import/`
# above (Godot's own cache), `.vscode/`/`.idea/` above (editor state). Nothing else needs a directory-shape
# rule; anything new that shows up untracked and ungoverned is gate 27's job to flag, not this file's job
# to pre-emptively hide.
#
# .claude/ still needs its own narrower rule: it holds machine-local session state (.DS_Store,
# scheduled_tasks.lock, worktrees/) alongside commands/, which must ship -- a slash command is a project
# asset the runtime re-reads every invocation, not a habit that degrades across sessions and compactions,
# so it belongs in the repo the same way .githooks/ does. This one stands alone now, not as a narrower
# case of the blanket rule above it.
/.claude/*
!/.claude/commands/

# Dev harness — generated screenshots (+ their Godot import sidecars)
/_capture*.png
/_capture*.png.import
_zoom_*.png
# The "Sees" tier canonical-moment captures (tools/capture_moments.gd) are TRACKED — see THE VISUAL RECORD
# below. (Was "tools/sees.sh", which has never existed in this repository.)
# Boot diagnostics and mock renders are not: they answer one question and stop mattering.
/_mock_*.png
/_mock_*.png.import
/_boot*.png

# Tool-script UIDs — tools/ scripts run by path (never referenced via uid://), so
# their UIDs are noise. Game-script UIDs (scenes/ src/ tests/) ARE committed.
tools/*.uid

# Same reasoning, legacy/tools/ specifically: legacy/ is frozen/read-only, but Godot's --import step
# still regenerates a missing .uid the moment it scans the tree, on every machine, every time — which
# showed up as 192 files of working-tree noise in a tree nobody is supposed to be editing. legacy/src/'s
# UIDs stay committed (same rule as scenes/ src/ tests/ above); legacy/tools/'s dev/QA scripts run by
# path like tools/ does.
legacy/tools/*.uid

# COUNT THEM WITH `git ls-files 'docs/media/moments/_moment_*.png' | wc -l`, NOT WITH `ls`, and do not
# write the answer down here. Three different numbers for this one set were once carried in three files
# at once, all of them stale, because a count in a comment is a test with no runner. The captures live
# under docs/media/, which carries a .gdignore, so the engine does not import them and they need no
# .import sidecars.
# THE VISUAL RECORD — history/ (the curated archive) and the canonical capture set — is
# COMMITTED, reversing an earlier decision on 2026-08-17. Both sat here ignored before, history/ under a
# note reading "NEVER publish or delete", which welded two unrelated things together. The LOCKED rule
# (docs/DECISIONS.md, "Never destroy a curated file") forbids DESTROYING them, and it exists
# because 84 of these screenshots were once purged during a refactor. Committing is the strongest
# available form of not destroying, so it serves that rule rather than breaking it; only the unbacked
# "publish" half is reversed, and a public MIT repo of one's own game discloses nothing by carrying
# pictures of it.
#
# Neither set is regenerable, whatever the old comment claimed. The game changes underneath them, so a
# capture is a record of a world that no longer exists — not an output you can rebuild by re-running
# anything. That is precisely why they needed an undo.
#
# The cost is written down because it is permanent: ~237MB (history/ 171MB, moments 66MB) against a .git
# that was 53MB. They are 8-bit RGB PNGs at 1920x1080, already ~3:1 zlib — there is no meaningful lossless
# win available, and lossy is barred outright because the harness SAMPLES these pixels (check_rock_reads
# and friends read values off them), so quantization would corrupt the evidence, not just soften it.
# A full recapture writes all 44 moments and adds ~66MB more; git stores per file, so a partial recapture
# costs only what actually changed.
#
# `history/.gdignore` keeps the archive out of Godot's resource scanner. CI runs `godot --headless --import`
# from a clean clone in BOTH jobs with no cache, so every tracked image is imported twice per push — and
# nothing references the archive, so importing 171MB of it is pure CI time. The moments stay visible to the
# scanner because tools/ addresses them by `res://` path (`save_png`, `Image.load`); that reads the file
# rather than the imported resource, but the path has to resolve.
#
# The archive's sidecars are stale the moment `.gdignore` lands — Godot will not refresh or recreate them,
# and they describe imports that no longer happen. Ignored rather than removed from disk, on the same
# principle as everything else in this file.
history/*.png.import

# docs/archive/session-exhaust/ — the SAME category as history/ above (archived, session-coordination
# review material; nothing in the live tree addresses it via res:// path — verified with a grep, not
# assumed), missing its own .gdignore until now. Without one, `godot --import` kept trying to import 88
# review screenshots (itm_review/, winch_review/) every run, and their tracked .import sidecars drifted
# out of sync with the tree the moment the files were archived (moved from docs/handoff/ to their current
# path without either landing a .gdignore or regenerating the sidecars) — found as ~90 files of unrelated
# working-tree noise mid-round (docs/DECISIONS_LEDGER.md D0098), not the change that caused it. Fixed the
# same way as history/: `docs/archive/session-exhaust/.gdignore` stops the scanner from touching the
# directory at all, and this ignores the sidecars going forward. `**` (not a flat `*`) because the archive
# has nested review subdirectories (winch_review/baseline/, winch_review/optionA/, ...), unlike history/'s
# flat layout — a plain `*` would miss those. The 88 already-tracked, now-permanently-stale copies are
# untracked with `git rm --cached` in the same commit, never `rm` — the files stay on disk.
docs/archive/session-exhaust/**/*.png.import

# Godot's per-machine ProjectSettings override + any local cert bundle it points at. Machine
# state, never project state: nothing here may change how the game builds for anyone else.
override.cfg
.local_ca_stub.pem

# Generated by tools/bake_miner.gd for eyeballing the frame set — not a shipped asset.
_miner_sheet.png
_miner_sheet.png.import

# Eyeball-only renders and the throwaway scripts that make them. Some questions the harness cannot answer —
# "does an 11px glyph read as a lightning bolt" is a thing a person looks at, not a thing a layer asserts —
# so a scratch script renders a sheet, somebody looks, and both are noise the moment the answer is known.
# Ignored rather than deleted, on the same principle as everything else here: excluding a file from the
# repo and removing it from the disk are different operations, and only one of them is reversible.
/_fixglyphs.png
/_fixglyphs.png.import
/_crop_*.png
/_crop_*.png.import
tools/_scratch_*.gd

# Same gap as legacy/tools/*.uid above, for the sibling pattern: this rule is root-anchored to tools/,
# so it stopped covering these files the moment `chore(pivot): move the pre-pivot codebase to legacy/`
# (4758d5a) relocated the whole tools/ directory to legacy/tools/ -- a directory move takes untracked,
# gitignored content with it for free, but nothing updates a root-anchored pattern to follow. Found when
# ~70 of these showed up as untracked noise in `git status` mid-session; confirmed via `git check-ignore`
# that this exact anchoring gap, not a live process writing into the frozen legacy/ tree, was the cause.
legacy/tools/_scratch_*.gd

# One-generation undo for the canonical captures (tools/capture_moments.gd). It was written when they were
# gitignored and it was the ONLY undo they had; now that they are committed, git holds every generation
# that was ever committed and this holds the one that was not — capture, look, recapture, all before any
# commit exists to fall back to. Still worth having, for a narrower reason than the one it was built for.
_moment_prev/

# Harness diagnostic frames. Distinct from the tracked _moment_*.png captures ON PURPOSE: a layer that
# writes into the canonical namespace dirties the tree on every run, and the ~38% run-to-run difference
# from animation phase alone means that churn is almost entirely noise being offered for commit.
_diag_*.png
_diag_*.png.import
# ...and the three that were committed under the canonical name before that split existed. Nothing writes
# them now, so they are frozen the moment the split landed — a tracked file that looks current and is not
# is worse than an absent one. Untracked with `git rm --cached`, never `rm`; the copies stay on disk.
/_moment_water.png
/_moment_water_body.png
/_moment_water_rock.png

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WORKING NOTES — kept on disk, kept out of the published tree.
#
# Day-to-day notes rather than documentation: running logs, review responses,
# scratch write-ups. They record HOW the work went rather than WHAT was built,
# and this is a public repository. Untracked with `git rm --cached`, never
# `rm` — every file is still on disk at its original path, which is the rule
# the rest of this file already follows.
#
# Nothing load-bearing reads these paths. Every inbound reference found was
# prose in a markdown file or a comment in a .gd/.sh source; no tool, no CI
# job, and no harness layer resolves one as a file. `tools/check_encoding.gd`
# walks the FILESYSTEM (DirAccess) rather than git, so it still scans these
# for mojibake exactly as before, and its CANARY (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md) is
# untouched.

/.rc-prose-*.patch

# Godot import sidecars for the capture set. The captures now live under docs/media/,
# which carries a .gdignore, so the engine no longer imports them and the sidecars
# describe nothing. Regenerated automatically if a capture is ever moved back.
/_moment_*.png.import
/_crop_*.png.import

# Scratch work lives here and only here (docs/ONBOARDING.md, "How to work"). If a scratch
# script turns out to be worth keeping, it becomes a real tool with a claim ID and moves out.
# The README explaining the directory's purpose is tracked; everything else in it is not.
tools/scratch/*
!tools/scratch/README.md

# Python bytecode cache, regenerated automatically by every gate script's own interpreter.
__pycache__/

# Two authored, working files under the otherwise-fully-tracked legacy/tools/, deliberately never
# committed. docs/archive/session-exhaust/handoff/CONVERGENCE_LEDGER.md (2026-08-23) has the original
# reasoning: they are session-coordination tooling (a director-bus protocol for multi-session work), and
# this is a public portfolio repository (CONTEXT.md) -- process internals don't belong in that tree, the
# same reason an earlier history rewrite removed their kind on purpose. Moved here from the local-only
# .git/info/exclude (ANVIL step 1, D0062/D0063): the reason is a project policy, not machine state, so a
# fresh clone should omit them too, not just this one.
legacy/tools/director_bus.sh
legacy/tools/test_director_bus.sh

# Staging area for proposals under director review, not yet a tracked decision -- currently
# incoming/ANVIL_ARCHITECTURE.md. Ignored rather than tracked or archived because neither is this
# session's call to make; flagged in ANVIL step 1's report as a follow-up disposition question (track
# once acted on, archive if rejected, or something else) rather than decided here.
/incoming/
